# Keep the build context small and the image reproducible.
#
# THE ONE TRAP THIS FILE EXISTS TO AVOID: excluding a workspace manifest. The
# Dockerfile runs `npm ci` against the root lockfile, and npm resolves the
# WHOLE workspace tree in one pass - so a missing `client/package.json` fails
# the install outright, even though nothing in the server image imports the
# client. All three of `shared/`, `server/` and `client/` package.json files
# must reach the context.

# node_modules especially: the image installs its own from the lockfile, and
# shipping a host's tree in would send hundreds of megabytes over the docker
# socket and risk platform-specific binaries built for the wrong OS.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build outputs. The image compiles its own; a stale local `dist` copied in
# would silently win over the one that was just built.
dist
**/dist

.git
.github
.vscode
.claude

# The CLIENT.
#
# It is deployed to Bloxity Hosting as static files and is never baked into the
# server image - but its package.json still has to reach the build context, for
# the reason at the top of this file.
#
# The client's CONTENTS are excluded, NOT the directory itself. `client` on its
# own excludes the directory, and re-including a file from underneath an
# excluded parent then depends on how the builder treats that parent - which is
# a subtlety to be right about rather than to rely on. `client/*` never
# excludes `client`, so there is no excluded parent to re-include across and
# the exception below cannot be ambiguous. It also fails SAFE: a builder that
# ignored the pattern entirely would merely produce a larger context, where a
# missing manifest breaks the build outright.
client/*
!client/package.json

# The game's art and audio. The SERVER never reads any of it - the world is
# pure data in `shared/`, and every texture is drawn on a canvas in the
# browser - so half a megabyte of mp3 has no business in a server image.
assets

# The verification suites run in CI against the repo, not inside the image.
scripts

# Player profiles are runtime state and belong on a mounted volume, never
# baked into a layer.
server/data

*.log
.env
.DS_Store
netlify.toml
README.md
CLAUDE.md
